  <arrangement id="aspace_6b0d7192754ea2149056c2f4b163654f">
    <head>Arrangement</head>
<p>The collection is arranged by subject into 12 series. Subject titles and dates are taken from information provided by the donor. Item numbers were assigned to photographs during processing. Included ephemera is not numbered.</p>  </arrangement>
  <bioghist id="aspace_6078bdff4f7539e5f4b1a4dd4ee0832b">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Paul J. Smith (September 8, 1931 – April 26, 2020) was an arts administrator, curator, artist, and photographer based in New York City. Born in Batavia, NY, in 1931, Smith worked in the art, craft, and design fields from the early 1950s onward. He was closely associated with the 20th century studio craft movement in the United States. Smith joined the staff of the American Craftsmen's Council (ACC, now the American Craft Council) in 1957, and in 1963 was appointed Director of the Museum of Contemporary Crafts in NYC, a position he held for the next 25 years. (The museum was later renamed the American Craft Museum, and it is now known as the Museum of Arts and Design.) In September 1987, Smith assumed the title of director emeritus at the museum and continued to work as an independent curator and consultant for museums, arts organizations, and collectors. Smith died in 2020 in New York City.</p>  </bioghist>
